            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Other Interests and Pursuits</strong><br>\nWhile primarily known for the telegraph, Morse continued to paint and engage in academic pursuits, contributing to the establishment of Vassar College and advocating for the arts throughout his life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Character</strong><br>\nMorse was noted for his devout Christian faith, which influenced both his professional and personal dealings. He had two marriages and numerous children, with his family life often intertwined with his work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nSamuel Morse passed away in 1872, leaving behind a legacy as the father of American telegraphy. His invention changed the world, ushering in a new era of communication and technology.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
